.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Predator and prey populations rise and fall periodically, out of phase. Every orbit is closed because the Lotka-Volterra invariant V = \delta x - \gamma\ln x + \beta y - \alpha\ln y is conserved: orbits are its level sets around the coexistence fixed point.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/ode_dynamics/population/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_lotka_volterra_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/ode_dynamics/population/plot_02_lotka_volterra`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Lotka-Volterra predator-prey cycles</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/ode_dynamics/population/plot_02_lotka_volterra`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Poincaré&#x27;s qualitative theory sorts a planar fixed point into one of four types -- node, saddle, spiral (focus), or center -- using only the eigenvalues of the linearized flow, without solving the equations. The eigenvalues depend only on the Jacobian&#x27;s trace \tau and determinant \Delta, so every type occupies its own region of the (\tau, \Delta) plane. This script classifies six canonical matrices, draws their phase portraits, and places each one on Poincaré&#x27;s trace-determinant diagram.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/ode_dynamics/stability/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_classification_zoo_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/ode_dynamics/stability/plot_01_classification_zoo`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Poincaré's classification of fixed points: nodes, saddles, spirals, centers</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/ode_dynamics/stability/plot_01_classification_zoo`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="For a stable linear system \dot x = Ax, solving the Lyapunov equation A^T P + PA = -Q gives an energy-like function V(x) = x^T P x whose level sets are ellipses that every trajectory crosses inward -- a proof of stability that never solves the ODE itself.">

